# Intent: a stop word that works while the robot is speaking (owner request 2026-10-21: he said "stop" mid-sentence and it kept talking). A small MultiNet command list (mn5q8_en: stop, stackchan stop, be quiet, quiet, that's enough, never mind, cancel) runs on the same echo-cancelled audio as the wake word, armed only while the device is in the Speaking state; a hit above 0.85 aborts the reply, which sends the host an abort so it ends the turn silently. Every hit and near-miss is reported to the host as a stop_word event with the fixed command name and score (never audio or words). Safety: the model is created at wake-word start-up and dropped for good if it costs more than 6 KB of internal RAM (the lowest free internal RAM seen was 12.5 KB), and arming is refused below 16 KB free internal RAM; both are logged and reported. The detection task's stack moves to PSRAM (12 KB) so the deeper detect() call costs no internal RAM. The build script now packs the multinet model beside the wakenet model, and xiaozhi keeps the AFE wake word when a multinet model is present (it used to switch to its custom wake word).
# Upstream: 78/xiaozhi-esp32 main/application.cc, main/audio/audio_service.{h,cc}, main/audio/wake_words/afe_wake_word.{h,cc}, scripts/build_default_assets.py (MIT). Applies after 048 with --unidiff-zero. recipe.sh selects CONFIG_SR_MN_EN_MULTINET5_SINGLE_RECOGNITION_QUANT8 and prunes the launcher icons so the assets fit the 4 MB partition.
# Check after a flash: the serial and host logs show "Stop word ready" (or "Stop word off" with the reason). While it reads a long answer, say "stop": the reply ends and the host log shows "unit stop word: ... accepted". With the robot talking and a TV on, "unit stop word" lines with accepted=false show the near-misses to tune.
